        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          When a self-propelled mower stops pulling, the instinct is to assume the transmission is gone and start pricing a new mower. Usually it is not. The drive system is a short chain of simple parts, a cable, a belt, a gearbox and two small gears in the wheels, and the cheap ones fail far more often than the expensive one. Check them in the order below and most mowers are driving again the same afternoon.
        </p>

        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How the Self-Propel Drive Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            On a typical gas walk-behind, the drive starts at the handle. Squeezing the drive bail or pressing a variable-speed control pulls a cable. At the other end, that cable either tightens a belt around a pulley or engages a clutch inside a small transmission. The belt runs from a pulley on the engine crankshaft to the transmission's input pulley, and the transmission turns an axle that drives either the front or the rear wheels.
          </p>

          <p>
            The last link is in the wheels themselves. Each end of the drive axle carries a small pinion gear that meshes with gear teeth molded inside the wheel. Between them sits a ratchet, usually a pair of spring-loaded pawls, that lets the wheels freewheel when you pull the mower backward and lock when the axle drives forward. That ratchet is also why most self-propelled mowers can still be pushed by hand when the drive fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery mowers replace the belt and often the transmission with a separate electric drive motor and gearbox, controlled by electronics that read the speed lever or handle sensor. The diagnostic logic is the same, working from control to wheel, but the middle of the chain is wiring and a motor rather than a belt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Knowing which link failed narrows the fix quickly. If nothing moves at all, look at the control end first: the cable, the belt or the interlocks. If the mower creeps or surges, think adjustment or a glazed belt. If one wheel drives and the other does not, the problem is almost always in that wheel. If you hear the drive working but the wheels do not move, the gears are slipping somewhere downstream.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
