            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="quick-checks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Quick Checks Before You Pick Up a Wrench
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the conditions, not the parts. A front-wheel-drive mower loses traction as the rear bag fills, because the bag's weight lifts load off the front wheels, and pressing down on the handle to steer makes it worse. If the drive fades over the course of a mow and recovers when you empty the bag, nothing is broken. Our comparison of <a href="/front-wheel-vs-rear-wheel-drive-lawn-mower/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">front-wheel vs rear-wheel drive mowers</a> explains why front drives struggle on slopes and full bags in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the variable-speed setting. Many mowers have a speed lever or dial separate from the drive bail, and if it has been knocked to its slowest position the mower can feel as if the drive has failed. The same goes for handle-pressure systems such as Toro's Personal Pace, where the speed depends on how hard you push the handle forward; a tired operator pushing gently gets a slow mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the wheels and the grass. Very tall or wet grass can overpower a light drive on any mower, especially a front-wheel-drive one. A wheel jammed with packed clippings, or a rear wheel height adjuster set lower than the front, can also make the mower feel as though it is dragging. Raise the cut a notch and try again on dry grass before diagnosing anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, make the mower safe before you go further. On a gas mower, pull the spark plug wire and keep it away from the plug. On a battery mower, remove the battery and the safety key. When you tip a gas mower to look underneath, follow the manual's direction, which is usually with the air filter and carburetor side up, so oil and fuel do not flood where they should not go.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FAST TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Watch the cable end while someone squeezes the bail.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">With the engine off, have a helper squeeze the drive control while you watch where the cable meets the transmission or belt idler. If that lever or arm does not move through its full travel, the problem is the cable. If it moves fully, look further down the chain.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Step One: The Drive Cable
          </h2>

          <p>
            The drive cable is the most common cause of a mower that stops self-propelling, and the cheapest to fix. Cables stretch with use, the outer housing compresses, and after a season or two the bail no longer pulls the inner wire far enough to fully engage the drive. The symptom is usually gradual: the mower first slips in thick grass, then needs the bail squeezed hard against the handle, then barely moves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most cables have an adjuster, either a threaded barrel near the handle or a clamp where the cable housing meets a bracket on the deck or handle. The general method is to loosen the lock nut or clamp, take up the slack until the bail engages the drive near the end of its travel, and lock it down again. Manufacturers specify how much free play to leave; Honda, for example, calls for only a few millimeters of free play at the lever on its walk-behinds, and Toro gives a model-specific range for its Personal Pace cable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not overtighten. A cable with no free play can hold the drive partly engaged all the time, which makes the mower hard to pull backward, wears the belt, and on handle-pressure systems can cause the drive to behave erratically. A good test, commonly used on Honda's Smart Drive models, is to pull the mower backward gently and then touch the drive control: a correctly adjusted drive should catch almost immediately, and let go cleanly when you release it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If adjustment does not help, inspect the cable. Squeeze the bail and watch the inner wire slide; if it is frayed, kinked, rusted inside its housing or snapped at the end fitting, replace it. A replacement cable for most brands costs about $15 to $35 and takes fifteen to thirty minutes. Take a photo of the routing before you remove the old one, because cables that are routed around the wrong side of a handle bolt bind and wear out quickly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Step Two: The Drive Belt
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the cable moves the drive linkage through its full travel and the mower still will not go, the belt is next. On most rear-wheel-drive gas mowers it runs under a plastic cover on top of the deck or behind the rear of the deck, from the engine crankshaft pulley to the transmission pulley. On many front-wheel-drive models it runs underneath, beside the blade, and you will need to tip the mower to see it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for four things. A belt that has jumped off its pulley is often fine and simply needs to be refitted, though you should find out why it came off. A glazed belt, shiny and hard on its sides, slips under load and needs replacing. A cracked, frayed or stretched belt that sits low in its pulley grooves is worn out. And a belt surrounded by packed grass and dirt may be fine but unable to grip, or may have been forced off by the buildup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clearing the debris is free and often solves the problem by itself. Wet clippings collect around the transmission pulley and belt guides, especially on mowers that are put away dirty, and that mat can push the belt off or stop the tensioner from moving. Scrape it out with a plastic putty knife, check that the belt guides and keepers are still in place, and refit the belt so it sits fully in both grooves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacement belts usually cost $15 to $40, and the exact part number matters. A belt that is even slightly too long will slip no matter how well the cable is adjusted, and auto-parts-store belts of nominally the same size often have a different profile. Order by the mower's model and serial number from the manufacturer's parts lookup or a dealer, and check the idler spring while you are in there, because a weak or unhooked spring produces exactly the same slipping symptom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Front-wheel-drive and rear-wheel-drive mowers differ in how they fail here. Front-drive belts sit closer to the blade and to flying debris, and they are often harder to reach. Rear-drive belts usually live under a cover and stay cleaner, but they carry more load because a rear-wheel drive is doing more of the work on hills. If a rear-drive belt keeps wearing quickly, check that the transmission is not dragging.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wheels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Step Three: Wheel Gears and Drive Pawls
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the belt and cable are fine and the axle is turning, but one or both wheels are not, the problem is at the wheel. This is the classic failure on older rear-wheel-drive mowers from mass-market brands, and the giveaway is that one wheel drives while the other just rolls along, or that you hear a ratcheting click from a wheel when the drive is engaged.
          </p>

          <p>
            To check, remove the hubcap and the wheel bolt, and slide the wheel off. Inside you will see a ring of gear teeth molded into the wheel, a pinion gear on the end of the axle, and on many designs a pair of small pawls with springs. Plastic wheel teeth wear smooth over years of use, pinions lose their teeth, and pawl springs weaken or go missing, so the pawls no longer catch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare the two sides. Worn teeth look rounded or stepped instead of square, and on a badly worn wheel you can see where the pinion has been skipping. If one wheel is visibly worn, replace both drive wheels together, because the second is usually not far behind and the mower will pull to one side with mismatched grip. Replacement drive wheels for common models cost about $15 to $30 each, and pinion gears and pawls are a few dollars each.
          </p>

          <p>
            While the wheels are off, clean out the hub and the axle end. Grass and grit packed into the pawl area can stop them from engaging even when nothing is worn. A light film of grease on the pinion and pawls, wiped of excess so it does not collect dirt, is fine. Reassemble with the pawls seated correctly; installed backward, they will let the wheel freewheel in the forward direction.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transmission" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Step Four: The Transmission, and Battery Drive Motors
          </h2>

          <p>
            The transmission is the last thing to suspect, not the first. You can confirm it fairly simply: with the belt correctly installed and the cable engaging the drive, if the transmission's input pulley spins but the axle does not turn, or turns without force, the transmission is slipping internally. Many consumer-mower transmissions are sealed units that are replaced rather than repaired.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices vary widely. A replacement gearbox for some common models, such as certain 22-inch Toro Recyclers, costs under $100, while others run to a couple of hundred dollars or more, and a few mass-market units are priced so close to a new mower that replacement makes no sense. Look up your exact part before starting, because the price often decides the question for you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some transmission failures are really adjustment failures. On handle-pressure designs such as Toro's Personal Pace, a cable adjusted too tight is a well-known cause of erratic drive and can wear the gearbox prematurely, so check that first. On any mower, a transmission that drags when the drive is off, making the mower hard to pull backward, points to a cable holding it partly engaged or to internal wear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery mowers are a different case. If the blade spins but the drive does nothing, check that the handle is fully latched and any folding-handle interlocks are engaged, that the speed control is not set to zero, and that the battery is charged. If the mower shows a fault light or code, look it up in the manual. When those checks pass, the drive motor or its controller has usually failed, and there is no adjustment to make; owners of EGO mowers, for example, have documented the whole motor and gearbox unit being replaced under warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes the warranty your first stop with a battery mower. Many battery brands cover their mowers for several years, and opening the drive housing can void that coverage. Firmware is rarely the fix on a walk-behind, since few models can be updated at all, but ask support whether there is a known issue before you buy parts. Our <a href="/best-battery-powered-lawn-mower/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best battery-powered lawn mower guide</a> notes which brands have the longest coverage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Parts Cost, and When to Replace the Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most self-propel repairs are cheap. A cable adjustment costs nothing, a cable or belt costs well under $50, and a pair of drive wheels is usually $30 to $60. Even a shop visit for a belt or cable is typically a modest bill compared to a new mower. If your mower otherwise runs well, any of these is worth doing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The calculation changes with the transmission. If the replacement gearbox costs more than about a third of a new mower's price, and the mower is old enough that the engine, deck and wheels are also tired, put the money toward a replacement. A rusted-through deck or an engine that is burning oil makes the decision even easier. Gas self-propelled mowers from good brands commonly cost several hundred dollars new, so do the arithmetic before ordering parts.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do replace it, use the failure as information. A front-wheel-drive mower that kept losing traction on your slope should be replaced with a rear-wheel or all-wheel drive model; our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-hills/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best lawn mower for hills guide</a> covers those. If you are choosing between fuel types, our <a href="/best-gas-lawn-mowers/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">gas lawn mower picks</a> and our main <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best self-propelled lawn mower guide</a> rank the current options.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your lawn has grown beyond what a walk-behind should cover, roughly an acre or more, it may be time to look at riders instead. Our sibling comparison of <a href="/zero-turn-vs-riding-mower/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">zero turn vs riding mower</a> explains which suits which yard. And whichever mower you end up with, our guide to <a href="/how-to-winterize-a-lawn-mower/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">winterizing a lawn mower</a> includes cleaning the deck and drive area, which prevents the packed-grass belt problems described above.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What It Usually Costs</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cable adjustment</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$15-40</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Drive belt</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$15-30</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Drive wheel, each</p>
              </div>
            </div>
