        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a transmission first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The transmission is the most expensive part in the drive and the least likely to have failed. A stretched cable or slipping belt produces the same no-drive symptom for a fraction of the price. Watch the cable end move, inspect the belt and check the wheels before ordering a gearbox, or you may fit one and still have the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overtightening the drive cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Taking out every bit of slack seems like the thorough fix, but a cable with no free play can hold the drive partly engaged. The mower becomes hard to pull backward, the belt wears faster and handle-pressure systems turn erratic. Leave the free play your manual specifies and test by pulling the mower backward.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fitting a generic belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drive belts look interchangeable, but small differences in length or profile make a belt slip or jump off. Order the part number for your model and serial, not a nominal size from an auto parts store. Check the idler spring and belt keepers at the same time, because a weak spring causes identical slipping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing only the worn wheel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When one drive wheel's gear teeth have worn smooth, the other is rarely far behind. Replacing just one leaves the mower pulling to one side and means taking it apart again next season. Replace both drive wheels together and inspect the pinions and pawls on both ends of the axle while they are off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working on it without disabling it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pulled drive belt or a tipped deck puts your hands next to the blade. Always pull the spark plug wire on gas mowers, or remove the battery and safety key on battery models, before reaching underneath. Tip gas mowers with the air filter and carburetor side up so oil and fuel stay where they belong.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my self-propelled mower stop self-propelling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most common cause is a drive cable that has stretched so the bail no longer fully engages the drive. Next comes a worn, glazed or jumped belt, often caused by packed grass. After those, look at worn gear teeth inside the drive wheels and, last, the transmission. Battery mowers more often have an interlock, controller or drive motor fault.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I adjust the cable on a self-propelled mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Find the adjuster, either a threaded barrel near the handle or a clamp where the cable housing meets a bracket. Loosen the lock nut, take up slack until the drive engages near the end of the bail's travel, then tighten. Leave the free play your manual specifies, and test by pulling the mower backward and touching the drive control.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my mower's drive belt is bad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a belt that is shiny and hard on the sides, cracked, frayed or riding low in the pulley grooves, or one that has come off entirely. A belt that slips in thick grass but works on short grass is often glazed or stretched. Replace it with the exact part for your model and serial number.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does only one wheel drive on my self-propelled mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always because the gear teeth inside that wheel, the pinion on the axle end, or the small ratchet pawls between them are worn or broken. Pull the wheel and compare it with the other side. Replacement drive wheels for common models cost about $15 to $30 each, and it is best to replace both together.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth fixing the self-propel on a lawn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Cable adjustments are free and cables, belts and drive wheels cost well under $50 each. A transmission is the exception: if the part costs more than about a third of a new mower and the rest of the machine is worn, put the money toward a replacement instead. Price your exact part first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my self-propelled mower slow going uphill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Front-wheel-drive mowers lose traction on slopes and with a full bag, because weight shifts off the front wheels. That is normal, not a fault. A rear-wheel-drive mower that slows uphill more than it used to may have a stretched cable or glazed belt. For steep yards, rear-wheel or all-wheel drive is the better design.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why won't my battery mower self-propel when the blade works?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the blade spins, the battery is delivering power, so check that the handle is fully latched, any folding interlocks are engaged and the speed control is not set to zero. Look up any fault light in the manual. If those check out, the drive motor or its controller has likely failed, which is usually a warranty repair.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

